// 121 — Functions as map/filter/reduce arguments — DESIGN NOTE
// In a language with first-class functions, the standard library
// hands you `map(f, xs)`, `filter(p, xs)`, `reduce(f, xs, init)` —
// you supply the per-element callable, the library does the walk.
//
// AiLang has no first-class function values (see 103). The walk and
// the per-element op live in the same place, the source: a WhileLoop
// over the data, with the operation written inline in the body.
//
// The trade is one less layer of indirection. You can see what the
// loop does by reading the loop. There's no "map's body is N
// definitions away in another file." Composition is by sequencing,
// not by chaining higher-order calls.
//
// 122 (map), 123 (filter), 124 (reduce) walk through the patterns.
SubRoutine.Main {
    PrintMessage("see 122_map_pattern, 123_filter_pattern, 124_reduce_pattern\n")
    PrintMessage("the AiLang form of each is an explicit WhileLoop, not a passed callback\n")
}
RunTask(Main)
